gm-method (the EXACT fix when CPU can't bit-match a GPU shader): when a physics collider / placement must match a GPU-rendered procedural surface EXACTLY and CPU float emulation can't (GPU float32+FMA+vendor-reordering, proven unportable), the clean exact path is an OFFLINE BAKE ARTIFACT: drive the real GPU shader's height-probe from a headless-chromium CLI (CDP, --use-angle=d3d11 real GPU or =swiftshader deterministic software) over the consumer's own coordinate grid, drain the pipelined probe properly (warm-up burst + K consecutive identical reads), and write a regular-grid Float32 artifact + metadata (anchor, extent, resolution, radius, seed). The consumer reads it with bilinear interpolation, GATED to fall back to the live CPU sampler outside the baked region / when absent. Result: collider == rendered surface to interpolation error (0.000 at grid nodes, sub-0.25m between 16m nodes; tighter with bake res) vs the ~1.3m CPU-float wall - AND a perf bonus (baked bilinear ~1000ms->4ms vs the CPU fractal build). Bake over the consumer's localToDir(x,z) grid directly (one probe/point, bake-time only) to AVOID the renderer's internal cube-face tile-param math (ox/oy/l face-metres = error-prone). Pair it with a CLI parity oracle (same CDP harness, batch CPU-vs-GPU) so any rollout is repeatably A/B-validated from node without manual browser-witness. CAVEAT: a fixed bake covers a fixed region (arena-fine); streaming/explore-far needs a larger or per-chunk bake (log the bound). Re-bake when the shader/anchor/radius/scale/seed change.